WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.957 --> 00:00:22.428
Having provided bread for my family, I began to think again about the wreck and all the valuables on board.

2
00:00:22.429 --> 00:00:25.877
Most of all I wanted to acquire the beautiful little sailboat.

3
00:00:25.878 --> 00:00:29.171
But I knew I would need a lot of help to do so.

4
00:00:29.172 --> 00:00:33.779
I began to coax my wife to let me take all the boys except Franz.

5
00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:39.688
"I'll consent to this," she finally said, "but you must promise me one thing."

6
00:00:39.689 --> 00:00:41.944
"Anything," I said eagerly.

7
00:00:41.945 --> 00:00:46.040
"You'll never spend another night on that dangerous wreck."

8
00:00:46.041 --> 00:00:47.836
"I promise."

9
00:00:47.837 --> 00:00:53.541
The three boys were delighted to go along.

10
00:00:53.542 --> 00:00:58.402
At Safety Bay we fastened on our life preservers and stepped into the tub boat.

11
00:00:58.403 --> 00:01:02.587
With the raft in tow, we steered straight for the wreck.

12
00:01:02.588 --> 00:01:06.700
On board I directed the boys to collect some provisions and load the raft.

13
00:01:06.701 --> 00:01:12.322
Meanwhile I studied the sailboat parts, which were stowed in a small compartment in the hold.

14
00:01:12.323 --> 00:01:16.751
The space was so narrow that it would be impossible to put the pieces together there.

15
00:01:16.752 --> 00:01:21.550
But the individual parts were so heavy that we couldn't move them to a bigger space.

16
00:01:21.551 --> 00:01:23.062
"Room to work in, boys!

17
00:01:23.063 --> 00:01:27.675
That's what we need first," I said when they came to see what I was doing.

18
00:01:27.676 --> 00:01:31.143
"Fetch axes and let's break down the walls around the sailboat."

19
00:01:31.144 --> 00:01:35.983
We worked hard, but as evening approached, we'd made little progress.

20
00:01:35.984 --> 00:01:38.832
Leaving the wreck, we headed for Safety Bay.

21
00:01:38.833 --> 00:01:43.701
Imagine our surprise when we found my wife and Franz waiting for us at Tentholm.

22
00:01:43.702 --> 00:01:46.885
She had decided to live there while we worked on the wreck.

23
00:01:46.886 --> 00:01:52.341
"That way you'll live nearer your work, and I won't lose sight of you," she said.

24
00:01:52.342 --> 00:01:54.397
I was delighted by her plan.

25
00:01:54.398 --> 00:02:06.196
"We'll work with great speed, so you may soon return to your beloved Falconhurst."

26
00:02:06.197 --> 00:02:09.727
We spent our days clearing an open space around the sailboat.

27
00:02:09.728 --> 00:02:12.421
Then we worked on putting the parts together.

28
00:02:12.422 --> 00:02:18.265
We started early and returned at night, always bringing valuable cargo from the old wreck.

29
00:02:18.266 --> 00:02:21.239
At last the sailboat was ready to be launched.

30
00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:25.146
But it was still imprisoned within massive wooden walls.

31
00:02:25.147 --> 00:02:30.642
We had been able to free the boat from its compartment, but we couldn't break through the ship's hull.

32
00:02:30.643 --> 00:02:34.669
Now it seemed that our work would end in failure and disappointment.

33
00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:38.475
I was almost in despair when an idea suddenly came to me.

34
00:02:38.476 --> 00:02:43.990
I took a large cannon, filled it with gunpowder, and added a long fuse.

35
00:02:43.991 --> 00:02:48.818
Then I placed the cannon so that it would blow out the side of the ship when it exploded.

36
00:02:48.819 --> 00:02:52.334
After the boys were in the tub boat, I lit the fuse.

37
00:02:52.335 --> 00:02:55.671
My heart was pounding as I hurried after them.

38
00:02:55.672 --> 00:03:00.325
We sailed back to Safety Bay, where we began unloading the day's haul.

39
00:03:00.326 --> 00:03:04.421
No one noticed my anxiety as I waited for the sound of the explosion.

40
00:03:04.422 --> 00:03:09.766
And then it came—a mighty roar! A grand burst of smoke!

41
00:03:09.767 --> 00:03:15.726
Terror-stricken, my wife and children looked first toward the sea and then at me.

42
00:03:15.727 --> 00:03:20.541
"Perhaps you left a light burning near some gunpowder," suggested my wife.

43
00:03:20.542 --> 00:03:24.543
"That's possible," I said quietly. "I'll go see what's happened.

44
00:03:24.544 --> 00:03:26.533
Will anyone come with me?"

45
00:03:26.534 --> 00:03:28.969
The boys sprang into the tub boat.

46
00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:32.696
The wreck looked the same from the side where we usually approached.

47
00:03:32.697 --> 00:03:35.129
But then we pulled around to the other side.

48
00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:39.317
There was a huge hole in the side of the ship, and the decks were torn apart.

49
00:03:39.318 --> 00:03:42.897
"Hurrah!" I cried. "The sailboat is ours!"

50
00:03:42.898 --> 00:03:45.282
The boys looked at me in surprise.

51
00:03:45.283 --> 00:03:48.320
"You planned that explosion!" said Ernest.

52
00:03:48.321 --> 00:03:52.734
"Yes, I did," I admitted. "But let's not tell Mother or Franz.

53
00:03:52.735 --> 00:03:56.213
I want them to be surprised when we sail into the bay."

54
00:03:56.214 --> 00:03:59.906
I had put rollers beneath the sailboat before the explosion.

55
00:03:59.907 --> 00:04:04.345
So with levers and pulleys we were able to gently lower the boat into the water.

56
00:04:04.346 --> 00:04:10.046
The boys spent the next few days helping me rig the boat and mount the two little brass cannons.

57
00:04:10.047 --> 00:04:14.480
Casting off from the wreck and spreading our sail, we began our voyage.

58
00:04:14.481 --> 00:04:19.145
The sailboat glided swiftly through the water. I stood at the helm.

59
00:04:19.146 --> 00:04:22.972
Ernest and Jack manned the cannons while Fritz gave the signal.

60
00:04:22.973 --> 00:04:25.122
"Fire!" he cried.

61
00:04:25.123 --> 00:04:32.828
Bang! Bang! The sound of the cannons echoed among the cliffs, followed by our happy shouts.

62
00:04:32.829 --> 00:04:37.747
My wife and Franz greeted us onshore. Fritz helped them to come aboard.

63
00:04:37.748 --> 00:04:42.701
"I don't know whether to scold you or praise you," said Mother breathlessly.

64
00:04:42.702 --> 00:04:46.110
"First I was startled by the sight of this strange boat.

65
00:04:46.111 --> 00:04:50.791
Then I heard the cannons. If I hadn't recognized your shouts—"

66
00:04:50.792 --> 00:04:54.030
"But do you like our sailboat?" interrupted Jack.

67
00:04:54.031 --> 00:05:00.665
"I do indeed, you dear, horrid, wonderful boys." My wife gave Jack a hug.

68
00:05:00.666 --> 00:05:06.752
"But don't you ever scare me like that again!"
